  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday morning through next Sunday)\nIssued at 159 PM EST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nA notable shift in model guidance over the past ~24-36 hours has \nincreased uncertainty in the forecast for the second half of the \nweek into the upcoming weekend. Previous guidance had a shortwave \nejecting out of the southern Plains across the northern Gulf and out \ninto the Atlantic, with a strong ridge building across the Southeast \nin its wake. However, the latest shift in guidance has trended \ntowards the shortwave cutting off from the flow over the northeast \nGulf and strengthening into a closed low by late Wednesday into \nThursday. From there, guidance has the low very slowly lifting \nacross the Southeast through the weekend, potentially resulting in \nmore unsettled weather than previously forecast. \n\nThere are several sources of uncertainty with these cutoff low \npatterns, with the main one being how far north the associated \nmoisture and precipitation can spread. The recent trends suggest \nthat this moisture will spread up across North and Central GA late \nweek and linger through the weekend, but there is one other player \nin this...and some of you likely guessed it...'the wedge'. Surface \nhigh pressure over the Mid-Atlantic will send dry and stable low-\nlevel across Georgia. This will do its best to fight any moisture \npushing in from the low, which will be dependent on its track and \nstrength. Bottomline, the evolving pattern warrants close watching \ngiven the substantial shift from the previously dry forecast \nconditions. \n\nIn summary, factoring in the latest trends and uncertainty, North \nand Central Georgia is likely to see a slight warming trend for the \nsecond half of the week into next weekend, with potential for rain. \nHighs will likely climb back into the upper 60s to low 70s, with \novernight lows in the upper 40s to low 50s. Our latest deterministic \nforecast has around a 10-20% chance across east-Central GA from \nThursday through the weekend, but that will change in the coming \ndays (both coverage and chances), as confidence in the forecast \nincreases.",
